<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · war</p><h1 class="enc-title">India–Pakistan war of 1971</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1971">1971</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as third Indo-Pakistani war</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> war of 1971 was fought between India and Pakistan during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh-liberation-war">Bangladesh Liberation War</a>, from 3 to 16 December 1971. It began with Pakistan's Operation Chengiz Khan, preemptive aerial strikes on eight Indian air stations, after which India declared war and joined the conflict on the side of Bengali nationalist forces. Thirteen days later the Eastern Command of the Pakistan military surrendered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhaka">Dhaka</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-pakistan">East Pakistan</a> became the new nation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>; approximately 93,000 Pakistanis were taken prisoner. Pakistani forces and the Razakars killed an estimated 300,000 to 3,000,000 civilians in Bangladesh, and eight to ten million people fled to India.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The war grew out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh-liberation-war">Bangladesh Liberation War</a>. Political tension in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-pakistan">East Pakistan</a> went back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partition-of-india">partition of India</a> in 1947 and was fed by the language movement of 1950, riots in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-bengal">East Bengal</a> in 1964 and mass protests in 1969, which led President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayub-khan">Ayub Khan</a> to resign and hand the central government to General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yahya-khan">Yahya Khan</a>. East Pakistan lay more than 1,600 kilometres from the western wing.</p><p>As reports spread of the Pakistani military's violence against Bengali civilians, large numbers of refugees crossed into eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, where camps were set up along the border, straining India's economy. By the end of April 1971 Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indira-gandhi">Indira Gandhi</a> asked the army chief, General Sam Manekshaw, whether he was ready for war. By his own account he declined, citing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> and tanks under refit, and asked to prepare on his own terms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On the evening of 3 December, at about 17:35, the Pakistan Air Force struck eight Indian airfields, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a>, 480 kilometres from the border. The raids, named Operation Chengiz Khan, were inspired by Israel's Operation Focus in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/six-day-war">Six-Day War</a>, but Pakistan flew too few aircraft to do significant damage. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> then declared war. Pakistan attacked on the western front, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-army">Indian Army</a> penetrated Pakistani territory there, taking about 15,010 square kilometres.</p><p>The Pakistan Air Force turned to defence, and its sorties fell day by day, while the Indian Air Force flew 4,000. On 16 December 1971 Lieutenant General Jagjit Singh Aurora of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s Eastern Command and Lieutenant-General A. A. K. Niazi of Pakistan's Eastern Command signed the instrument of surrender at the Ramna Race Course in Dacca. Crowds shouted anti-Pakistan slogans, and Indian officers formed a human chain to shield Niazi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> under the Indo-Soviet Treaty of Friendship and Cooperation of August 1971, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> criticised India's role. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheikh-mujibur-rahman">Sheikh Mujibur Rahman</a> returned to Dacca and on 19 January 1972 became the first President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimla">Simla</a> on 2 July 1972 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulfikar-ali-bhutto">Zulfikar Ali Bhutto</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indira-gandhi">Indira Gandhi</a> signed an agreement under which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> recognised Bangladesh's sovereignty in return for its prisoners, whom India released over five months. Pakistan lost more than half its population and set up a War Enquiry Commission under Chief Justice Hamoodur Rahman.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “India–Pakistan war of 1971” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
